.TH smi-backup "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Workstation Command"
.SH NAME
smi-backup \- Back up the important home directories of a developer machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-backup
.I TARGET_DIRECTORY
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Creates
.IR TARGET_DIRECTORY/TIMESTAMP/ ,
the timestamp coming from
.BR smi-timestamp (1),
and packs each backed up directory into its own
.B .tar.gz
archive there, named after the directory with the leading dot removed.
.PP
Backed up, when present; a missing directory is skipped without an error:
.PP
.RS
.I ~/.gnupg*
(every GnuPG directory, backups and robot keyrings included),
.I ~/.ssh ~/.pki ~/.dev-certs ~/.revelation ~/.mozilla ~/.thunderbird ~/Backup ~/Documents
.I ~/Pictures ~/Music ~/Videos ~/sources ~/.config ~/.local/share/TelegramDesktop
.RE
.PP
The shell login files
.IR ~/.profile ,
.IR ~/.bash_profile ,
.IR ~/.bash_login ,
.I ~/.bashrc
and
.I ~/.bash_logout
are packed together into a single
.IR dotfiles.tar.gz .
Only the ones this machine actually has are packed, so a Debian home with
.I ~/.profile
and a Rocky Linux or Fedora home with
.I ~/.bash_profile
are both backed up whole and neither reports a missing file.
.PP
.I ~/Backup
is where password manager databases such as KeePass
.B .kdbx
files are kept, so they are covered.
.PP
.I ~/sources
is packed without build garbage: every
.IR target ,
.I node_modules
and
.I .venv
directory is left out of the archive.
.PP
.I ~/.config
holds application settings and messaging data, including
.B Signal
under
.IR ~/.config/Signal .
The browser and Electron cache directories under it
.RB ( Cache ,
.BR "Code Cache" ,
.BR GPUCache ,
.BR "Service Worker" ,
.B Crashpad
and similar) are excluded: they are large and disposable, and the excludes touch only caches,
never the settings, messages or profiles beside them.
.I ~/.local/share/TelegramDesktop
holds Telegram Desktop message data and is packed whole.
.PP
Nothing is deleted from the live tree, every exclusion happens during packing.
.PP
A file that changes or vanishes while it is being read, common in the config of a running
application, is a warning, not a failure. Any harder error stops the command, so a partial backup
is never mistaken for a complete one.
.PP
Symbolic links are never followed: a link is stored as a link, its target's content is not
packed.
A backed up directory that is itself a symbolic link therefore yields an archive holding only
the link.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I TARGET_DIRECTORY
Existing directory the timestamped backup directory is created under.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All present directories were archived.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or nonexistent target directory, or an archive failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.B smi-backup /run/media/has/usb-disk
.br
.B smi-backup /var/backups
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR smi-timestamp (1),
.BR tar (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Imre Tabur <imre.tabur@mail.ee>
